# Upstream hermes-agent v0.16.0 dependencies — awaiting upstream fix
# PyJWT 2.12.1 → 2.13.0 (auth bypass via forged JWTs)
CVE-2026-48526
# Starlette 1.0.1 → 1.1.0 (SSRF via UNC paths in StaticFiles)
CVE-2026-48818
# Starlette 1.0.1 → 1.3.1 (form limits silently ignored, DoS)
CVE-2026-54283

# mcp 1.26.0 -> 1.27.2/1.28.1 (MCP Python SDK: HTTP transport session handling,
# experimental task handlers, WebSocket transport). HELD, not fixable here:
# hermes-agent 0.19.0 pins mcp==1.26.0 and is written against the 1.x client
# API. Forcing the fix resolves to mcp 2.1.1 (the MCP 2.x SDK) and breaks the
# import Kagetora depends on:
#   ImportError: cannot import name 'streamablehttp_client'
#                from 'mcp.client.streamable_http'
# Verified by the build-time smoke test, not assumed. Mitigation: the agent runs
# on an internal=true network with no direct egress and reaches every tool
# through the Trentina gateway, so the HTTP/WebSocket transport surface these
# CVEs describe is not exposed. Upstream resolves this in 0.20.x via their MCP
# 2.x SDK migration -- revisit when 0.20/0.21 reach PyPI.
CVE-2026-52869
CVE-2026-52870
CVE-2026-59950

# pip's vendored bundle: msgpack 1.1.2 and setuptools/pkg_resources 70.3.0.
#
#   GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964  msgpack     OOB read on Unpacker reuse
#   CVE-2025-47273       setuptools  path traversal in PackageIndex
#
# These are NOT the packages Hermes uses. The real ones are patched in the
# Containerfile and scan clean -- msgpack 1.2.2, setuptools 84.0.0. What Trivy
# reports here is /app/venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/_vendor/, which
# ships its own frozen copies (confirmed directly in the built image: the
# vendored msgpack/__init__.py declares version 1, 1, 2, and pkg_resources sits
# alongside it). They appear under Trivy's aggregate "Python" target with no
# path in the report summary, which is why they looked like phantoms and
# survived three wrong theories -- a duplicated lib64 tree, layer history, and
# a stale CI cache. All three were wrong; it was pip's vendor directory.
#
# Not fixable by upgrading: the versions are frozen inside pip itself. Deleting
# pip is not safe either -- Hermes can lazy-install skill dependencies at
# runtime. Both are only reachable through pip's own HTTP cache path, i.e. only
# if pip talks to a package index, and this container has no direct egress
# (internal=true network, every tool via the Trentina gateway).
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273
